## 4. ALGEBRAIC ERROR CORRECTION OVER \(\mathbb{F}_{16}\)

### 4.1 Field Construction

We work in the Galois field \(\mathbb{F}_{16}\), constructed as

\[
\mathbb{F}_{16} = \mathbb{F}_2[x] \,/\, (x^4 + x + 1).
\]

The polynomial \(x^4 + x + 1\) is verified primitive over \(\mathbb{F}_2\); let \(\alpha\) denote a root, so \(\alpha^4 = \alpha + 1\). Elements of \(\mathbb{F}_{16}\) are 4‑bit vectors; arithmetic is defined by this polynomial. The field axioms, the minimal polynomial, and the primitive element \(\alpha\) are all encoded in Layer 3 of the grammar.

### 4.2 Reed–Solomon Code RS(15,11)

We employ a Reed–Solomon code with the following parameters:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Field:} &\quad \mathbb{F}_{16} \\
\text{Block length:} &\quad n = 15\ \text{symbols} \;(60\ \text{bits}) \\
\text{Message length:} &\quad k = 11\ \text{symbols} \;(44\ \text{bits}) \\
\text{Parity symbols:} &\quad n-k = 4 \;(16\ \text{bits}) \\
\text{Error correction:} &\quad t = 2\ \text{symbols} \;(8\ \text{bits}) \ \text{per block} \\
\text{Code rate:} &\quad R_{\text{code}} = 11/15 \approx 0.7333 .
\end{aligned}
\]

The generator polynomial is

\[
G(x) = (x-\alpha)(x-\alpha^{2})(x-\alpha^{3})(x-\alpha^{4}) \in \mathbb{F}_{16}[x] ,
\]

a degree‑4 polynomial whose coefficients are elements of \(\mathbb{F}_{16}\). Systematic encoding maps 11 message symbols to 15 codeword symbols by appending the remainder of the message polynomial divided by \(G(x)\).

### 4.3 Exact Budget Computation

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Available bits (rows 0–57):} &\quad 5858\ \text{bits} \\
5858/4 = 1464.5 &\quad\Rightarrow\ \text{pad to next multiple of 4 bits: } 5860\ \text{bits (2 zero bits)} \\
5860/4 = 1465\ \text{symbols} &\quad\Rightarrow\ \text{not divisible by 15} \\
\text{Pad to next multiple of 15 symbols:} &\quad 1470\ \text{symbols} = 5880\ \text{bits (20 more zero bits)} \\
\text{Total padding:} &\quad 22\ \text{bits of known zeros} \\
\text{RS blocks:} &\quad 1470/15 = 98\ \text{blocks exactly} \\
\text{Total message symbols:} &\quad 98 \times 11 = 1078\ \text{symbols} \\
\text{Decoded bits before stripping pad:} &\quad 1078 \times 4 = 4312\ \text{bits} \\
\text{Subtract 22 known padding bits:} &\quad 4290\ \text{bits net semantic payload}.
\end{aligned}
\]

A 6‑bit field at the very start of the decoded payload encodes the number of padding bits (22), enabling the receiver to strip them exactly. This field is itself part of the payload, leaving **4284 bits for the grammar layers** (the 6‑bit padding‑length field is treated as Layer 0 overhead). The grammar budget of Section 5 accounts for 4284 bits.

### 4.4 Interleaving for Burst Error Correction

A block interleaver of depth \(D = 15\) is applied to the RS‑encoded symbol stream before transmission. The 1470 symbols are written row‑wise into a \(15 \times 98\) matrix and read column‑wise. Any contiguous burst of up to 15 consecutive corrupted symbols is thereby distributed across 15 distinct RS blocks, each receiving at most one corrupted symbol from the burst. Since each RS block corrects up to \(t = 2\) symbol errors, the interleaved system corrects any burst of up to \(2 \times 15 = 30\) consecutive symbols (120 bits). At the stated symbol rate, 120‑bit bursts correspond to events lasting over 133 000 seconds (≈ 1.5 days), far beyond the coherence time of any known interstellar scintillation mechanism at 1.42 GHz for a source within 100 light‑years.

### 4.5 Decoding Threshold Verification

At \(E_s/N_0 = 35.5\ \text{dB}\) (Theorem 1), the raw BPSK bit error rate is

\[
p_b = \tfrac12 \operatorname{erfc}\!\bigl(\sqrt{E_b/N_0}\,\bigr)
     \approx \tfrac12 \operatorname{erfc}\!\bigl(\sqrt{35\,820 \times 11/15}\,\bigr)
     \approx 10^{-8000} .
\]

This lies so far below the RS(15,11) threshold that essentially zero symbol errors occur before decoding. The concatenated system operates in the exponential tail of the performance curve, and the failure probability over the entire 4290‑bit payload is less than \(10^{-9}\). ∎

