### Layer 3: Linear Algebra, Kronecker Product, and Graph Theory (≈ 640 bits)

**Matrices.** A matrix \(A\) of dimensions \(m\times n\) over a field \(\mathbb{F}\) is an array of \(m\times n\) elements of \(\mathbb{F}\), organised in \(m\) rows and \(n\) columns. Matrix addition and scalar multiplication are defined elementwise. Matrix multiplication of an \(m\times n\) matrix \(A\) by an \(n\times p\) matrix \(B\) produces the \(m\times p\) matrix \(C\) with \(C_{ij} = \sum_k A_{ik} B_{kj}\).

**The Kronecker product — explicit bootstrap.** For \(A \in \mathbb{F}^{m\times n}\) and \(B \in \mathbb{F}^{p\times q}\),

\[
(A \otimes B)_{(i-1)p+r,\,(j-1)q+s} = A_{ij} \times B_{rs}
\qquad (i=1,\dots,m;\; j=1,\dots,n;\; r=1,\dots,p;\; s=1,\dots,q).
\]

Equivalently, replace each scalar entry \(a_{ij}\) of \(A\) with the \(p\times q\) block \(a_{ij}\cdot B\) and read the resulting block matrix row‑major. This construction uses only scalar multiplication and block concatenation, both defined from matrix arithmetic above.

**Stochastic matrices.** A square matrix \(T\) of dimension \(d\times d\) over \(\mathbb{R}\) is stochastic if all entries are non‑negative and all row sums equal 1. \(T\) represents the transition probabilities of a finite‑state dynamical system.

**Stationary distribution.** For an ergodic stochastic matrix \(T\), the unique stationary distribution \(\pi\) is the probability vector satisfying \(\pi T = \pi\), with all \(\pi_i > 0\) and \(\sum_i \pi_i = 1\). This vector is the left eigenvector of \(T\) corresponding to eigenvalue 1, computable by power iteration or Gaussian elimination over \(\mathbb{R}\), both definable from Layer 2 arithmetic.

**Directed graphs.** A directed graph \(G = (V,E)\) is a pair of a finite vertex set \(V\) and an edge multiset \(E \subseteq V\times V\). The adjacency matrix, the transition matrix, and the concept of a bipartition \(B = (B_1,B_2)\) of \(V\) (a partition into two non‑empty disjoint subsets) are defined.

