**Kullback–Leibler divergence between transition matrices.** For two stochastic matrices \(T\) and \(Q\) of the same dimensions,

\[
D_{\text{KL}}(T \,\|\, Q \mid \pi)
   = \sum_{i=1}^{d} \sum_{j=1}^{d}
      \pi_i \, T_{ij} \, \ln\!\left(\frac{T_{ij}}{Q_{ij}}\right) ,
\]

with the convention \(0 \cdot \ln(0/q) = 0\) and the sum taken only over \((i,j)\) with \(T_{ij} > 0\). This is the expected log‑likelihood ratio of \(T\) versus \(Q\) under the stationary dynamics, a well‑defined non‑negative quantity. Every operation (summation, logarithm, expectation under \(\pi\)) is constructible from Layers 0–4.

**Independent subdynamics.** For a bipartition \(B = (B_1,B_2)\) of \(V\), let \(T|_{B_1}\) be the \(d_1\!\times\!d_1\) sub‑transition matrix (renormalised rows) describing dynamics restricted to \(B_1\), and similarly \(T|_{B_2}\). The independent dynamics of \(B_1\) and \(B_2\) is modelled by the Kronecker product

\[
T^{B} = T|_{B_1} \otimes T|_{B_2} \;\in\; \mathbb{R}^{d\times d}.
\]

**Integrated information.** The integrated information of \(\mathcal{S}\) is

\[
\Phi(\mathcal{S}) = \min_{B \in \mathcal{P}(V)}
   D_{\text{KL}}\bigl(T \,\|\, T^{B} \mid \pi\bigr) ,
\]

where the minimum is taken over all bipartitions \(\mathcal{P}(V)\) of \(V\) into two non‑empty parts. \(\Phi(\mathcal{S}) \ge 0\), with equality if and only if \(T\) factors exactly as a Kronecker product (the dynamics are causally decomposable). A high value of \(\Phi\) certifies that the global dynamics cannot be reduced to independent dynamics of any partition—it is irreducibly integrated.

**Theorem 3 (Universal computability of \(\Phi\)).** For any finite graph \(\mathcal{S}\), the quantity \(\Phi(\mathcal{S})\) is computable from the grammar axioms of Layers 0–4 via:

1. Computing the stationary distribution \(\pi\) (Layer 3: linear algebra).  
2. For each bipartition \(B\): computing \(T^{B}\) via the Kronecker product (Layer 3: explicit bootstrap).  
3. Computing \(D_{\text{KL}}(T \,\|\, T^{B} \mid \pi)\) via summation of logarithms (Layer 2: \(\ln\) defined; Layer 0: finite sums).  
4. Taking the minimum over all bipartitions (Layer 1: minimisation over a finite set).

The bipartition search is combinatorial (\(2^{d-1}-1\) bipartitions for a \(d\)-vertex graph) and finite for any finite graph. ∎

